<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- W3C SCXML 6.2 `<send>` `<param>` payload delivery on the two paths that
     had no runtime witness.

     Both were fixed at the template layer and both stayed unproven, for the
     same reason: no committed fixture had a machine of the required shape, so
     the suites could only show that nothing regressed. The absence is also
     why the defects survived — a shape nobody generates is a shape nobody
     breaks and nobody fixes.

     Two independent paths, deliberately reaching different final states so a
     failure names which one broke rather than reporting "payload lost":

       child -> parent, from a machine with no script engine
         The child needs no script engine at all: every one of its params
         is a literal, so each folds at codegen time. Kotlin
         used to gate param emission on the *machine* needing one, so this
         shape shipped a `<send>` with its params dropped. The right question
         is whether *this send* needs an engine, which is what the C++
         template had always asked. Reaching `passChild` means the payload
         crossed the invoke boundary from an engine-less child.

       #_internal, from a machine that does have an engine
         Sends to `#_internal` used to drop their payload unconditionally —
         the internal-raise path took no event data, so params were built and
         then discarded. Reaching `pass` means an internal raise now carries
         its params to the receiving transition's `_event.data`.

     The parent has to *read* the payload to judge it, so it needs an
     engine; the child only emits literals, so it needs none, and being
     engine-less is precisely the property under test. The two cannot be
     one machine.

     Both declare `datamodel="ecmascript"`. The child read `null` for as
     long as the attribute reached no decision, and that reading was
     wrong twice over: it is not what makes the child engine-less (its
     literal params are), and W3C SCXML B.1.4 gives the Null data model
     no value expression language for the `expr` attributes it carries.

     Values are strings on the first two paths: `<param>` payload marshalling
     is not the axis there, and a numeric literal would additionally assert how
     each backend maps number types across the boundary.

     A third phase asks exactly that, because nothing else did. Two claims,
     both about what a `<param>` VALUE is when it arrives:

       a number stays a number
         `expr="7"` is the Number 7, and `_event.data.n === 7` is how a
         document reads it. A backend that stringifies on the way through
         delivers "7", which `===` finds unequal — a guard that silently takes
         the other branch, which is the failure mode this axis keeps producing.

       two params of one name keep both values AND their type
         W3C SCXML 6.2 lets a name repeat, and test178 in the IRP corpus is
         MANUAL: the tester reads the log to confirm both pairs are there, so
         no automated channel has ever checked it. C++ folds duplicates into an
         array — measured 2026-08-16 in `EventDataHelper::mergeParams`, whose
         `values.size() > 1` branch publishes the STRING vector and drops the
         typed values it is holding at the same moment. The claim is the array
         AND its element types: `d.length === 2 && d[0] === 1`.

     A fourth phase asks what a `<param>` costs when its expression will not
     evaluate. W3C SCXML 5.7.1 answers with two halves — "MUST place the
     error 'error.execution' on the internal event queue and MUST ignore the
     name and value" — and measurement on 2026-08-21 found five of the seven
     channels keeping neither, each differently: C++ (both engines), Kotlin
     and C11 said nothing and shipped a placeholder under the param's name,
     and Python reported the error but discarded the entire message, so a
     document that miscomputed one param lost the whole event and stalled
     with nothing to act on. Rust and Go were already right.

     The clause that governs is 5.7.1 and not 6.2.3's "discard the message":
     that one is about `<send>`'s own arguments, and reading it over a
     `<param>` leaves "ignore the name and value" with nothing to mean —
     there would be no message for the name to be absent from. W3C test343
     settles it from the other side of the same clause: an illegal `<param>`
     under `<donedata>` raises `error.execution` AND the `done.state` event
     still arrives, with empty data. `DoneDataHelper::evaluateParams` had
     that reading written down all along; the five `<send>` sites had
     re-derived it and got it wrong.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="awaitChild"
       name="send_param_payload">

    <datamodel>
        <!-- Read by the fourth phase's `<param expr>`. Indexing `null` is a
             runtime error in every expression language this corpus runs —
             a TypeError in ECMAScript, an index-of-nil in Lua — which is
             what makes one document able to ask all seven channels the
             same question. -->
        <data id="nothing" expr="null"/>
        <!-- Set by the `error.execution` handler below. A counter rather
             than a state, because the phase has to still be standing when
             the send's own event arrives: the verdict is about what BOTH
             queues did, and moving states would answer only the first. -->
        <data id="sawParamError" expr="0"/>
    </datamodel>

    <state id="awaitChild">
        <invoke type="scxml" id="inv_emitter">
            <content>
                <scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
                       version="1.0" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="emit">
                    <state id="emit">
                        <onentry>
                            <send target="#_parent" event="fromChild">
                                <param name="value" expr="'42'"/>
                            </send>
                        </onentry>
                        <transition target="sent"/>
                    </state>
                    <final id="sent"/>
                </scxml>
            </content>
        </invoke>

        <transition event="fromChild"
                    cond="_event.data &amp;&amp; _event.data.value === '42'"
                    target="internalPhase"/>
        <!-- Same event, no cond: the payload did not arrive intact. -->
        <transition event="fromChild" target="failChildPayload"/>
    </state>

    <state id="internalPhase">
        <onentry>
            <send target="#_internal" event="loopback">
                <param name="carried" expr="'kept'"/>
            </send>
        </onentry>
        <transition event="loopback"
                    cond="_event.data &amp;&amp; _event.data.carried === 'kept'"
                    target="typedPhase"/>
        <transition event="loopback" target="failInternalPayload"/>
    </state>

    <!-- W3C SCXML 6.2: what a `<param>` value IS on arrival. The two claims
         are separated into two transitions so a failure names which one broke:
         a number that arrived stringified is a different defect from a
         duplicate name that lost one of its values. -->
    <state id="typedPhase">
        <datamodel>
            <!-- Read by a `<param expr>` below rather than written into one.
                 A param whose expression is a literal is folded at codegen
                 time and serialised there; only one that has to be EVALUATED
                 reaches the runtime serialiser, and until this variable
                 existed no string ever did — every string-valued param in
                 this corpus was a literal, so the serialiser's string arm
                 could emit any spelling at all and stay green (measured
                 2026-08-17 by a mutation that made it emit Lua quotes). -->
            <data id="tag" expr="'kept'"/>
        </datamodel>
        <onentry>
            <send target="#_internal" event="typed">
                <param name="n" expr="7"/>
                <param name="s" expr="tag"/>
                <param name="d" expr="1"/>
                <param name="d" expr="2"/>
            </send>
        </onentry>
        <transition event="typed" cond="_event.data.n !== 7" target="failNumberType"/>
        <transition event="typed" cond="_event.data.s !== 'kept'" target="failStringType"/>
        <transition event="typed"
                    cond="_event.data.d.length === 2 &amp;&amp; _event.data.d[0] === 1 &amp;&amp; _event.data.d[1] === 2"
                    target="paramErrorPhase"/>
        <transition event="typed" target="failDuplicateParams"/>
    </state>

    <!-- W3C SCXML 5.7.1: a `<param>` whose expression will not evaluate.
         Three claims, one send, each with its own verdict state so a failure
         names which half of the clause broke:

           the failure is reported
             `error.execution` reaches the internal queue, and it reaches it
             BEFORE the send's own event, because the clause places it there
             while the `<send>` is being evaluated. The handler is targetless
             so this phase is still standing when `withBadParam` arrives —
             that is what lets one state judge both queues.

           the offending pair is left out
             not carried with a placeholder value. A receiver that asks for
             the field has to find nothing, which is what "MUST ignore the
             name and value" means; four channels answered `""` or `nil`
             under the name instead, and `=== ''` is what separates those
             from an absent field in every expression language here.

           the rest of the message still goes
             `kept` arrives intact and `withBadParam` arrives at all. A
             channel that discards the whole message reaches no verdict
             state at all and times out — deliberately, because a stalled
             machine is exactly what the defect did to a document. -->
    <state id="paramErrorPhase">
        <onentry>
            <send target="#_internal" event="withBadParam">
                <param name="kept" expr="'here'"/>
                <param name="broken" expr="nothing.deep"/>
            </send>
        </onentry>
        <transition event="error.execution">
            <assign location="sawParamError" expr="1"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="withBadParam" cond="sawParamError !== 1" target="failNoParamError"/>
        <transition event="withBadParam" cond="_event.data.broken === ''" target="failBrokenParamDelivered"/>
        <transition event="withBadParam" cond="_event.data.kept !== 'here'" target="failSiblingParamLost"/>
        <transition event="withBadParam" target="pass"/>
    </state>

    <final id="pass"/>
    <final id="failChildPayload"/>
    <final id="failInternalPayload"/>
    <final id="failNumberType"/>
    <final id="failStringType"/>
    <final id="failDuplicateParams"/>
    <final id="failNoParamError"/>
    <final id="failBrokenParamDelivered"/>
    <final id="failSiblingParamLost"/>
</scxml>
